WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.668
Read a class diagram as a precise but selective
model.

00:00:03.668 --> 00:00:10.393
Identify classes, access levels, operations and
relationships, then interpret multiplicities in the

00:00:10.393 --> 00:00:10.869
domain.

00:00:10.869 --> 00:00:17.458
Use code examples to check the model, especially
when a collection’s capacity differs from actual

00:00:17.458 --> 00:00:18.205
membership.

00:00:18.205 --> 00:00:24.250
Ownership, inheritance and interface contracts
communicate different ideas, so choose the

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:27.511
relationship that matches the intended behavior.

00:00:27.511 --> 00:00:33.568
Patterns preserve design knowledge by naming
recurring problems and explaining solutions and

00:00:33.568 --> 00:00:34.424
consequences.

00:00:34.424 --> 00:00:37.255
Apply them with judgment, not mechanically.

00:00:37.255 --> 00:00:41.403
Selective diagrams can help communicate the relevant
structure.

00:00:41.403 --> 00:00:47.987
When comparing composition and inheritance, examine
the relationship and the behavior clients depend

00:00:47.987 --> 00:00:52.004
on; reuse alone is not enough to justify a subtype
hierarchy.

00:00:52.011 --> 00:00:55.948
Single responsibility concerns coherent reasons to
change.

00:00:55.948 --> 00:01:02.466
Use method relationships and shared fields as clues,
then ask which stakeholder or policy drives

00:01:02.466 --> 00:01:03.280
each change.

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:10.001
Splitting responsibilities can improve
maintainability, but excessive fragmentation can
obscure the

00:01:10.001 --> 00:01:10.408
model.

00:01:10.408 --> 00:01:17.197
Public libraries add compatibility costs, making
clear boundaries especially valuable before clients

00:01:17.197 --> 00:01:18.216
depend on them.
